Subject: [PATCH 4/4] bn: scope HAVE_BN_DIV_WORDS to the branch that makes it true

Independent of 0001/0002 (m4/libtool.m4 and generated `configure`) and of
0003 (configure.ac and crypto/Makefile.am.elf-x86_64); this one touches
only crypto/bn/arch/amd64/bn_arch.h. Any application order works.

## The defect

Building libressl with tcc as CC, with assembly ENABLED (which 0003 made
possible for the first time), fails to link:

    unresolved reference to 'bn_div_words'

libcrypto, libssl and libtls all link; apps/ is where it surfaces.

Nothing on amd64 defines bn_div_words. Not the 21 vendored s2n-bignum
routines, not arch/amd64/bn_arch.c. The only definition anywhere in the
tree is the portable C one at crypto/bn/bn_div.c:77, and it is compiled
under `#ifndef HAVE_BN_DIV_WORDS`.

So `#define HAVE_BN_DIV_WORDS` in this header is not the claim it looks
like. It does not mean "a faster bn_div_words is available here". It means
"nothing will call bn_div_words here" -- and that is true only when the
GNUC-gated bn_div_rem_words_inline below it is compiled, because bn_div.c's
own portable bn_div_rem_words_inline (line 155) is the sole caller in the
tree, and it exists only when HAVE_BN_DIV_REM_WORDS_INLINE is unset.

The define sat outside that conditional, gated only on !_WIN32 &&
!OPENSSL_NO_ASM. Any compiler that is not GNUC therefore got the caller
compiled in and the callee compiled out. gcc and clang never noticed
because they always take the branch.

This is latent upstream, not tcc-specific -- tcc is simply the first
non-GNUC compiler to build this tree with asm on. Every recorded tcc build
before now used --disable-asm, which sets OPENSSL_NO_ASM and short-circuits
the whole block, which is why it stayed hidden.

## The fix, part one: move the define inside the conditional

The define is moved to sit with HAVE_BN_DIV_REM_WORDS_INLINE, so it is
present exactly when the thing that justifies it is present. Any compiler
that does not take the branch now gets bn_div.c's portable bn_div_words
compiled in, matching its portable bn_div_rem_words_inline -- a coherent
pair, in either direction.

This half is what closes the bug generally, for any future non-GNUC
compiler, rather than for the one that happened to find it.

## The fix, part two: admit tcc to the branch

tcc supports GNU extended inline asm, and the three helpers in this block
are the plain register-constraint forms: `divq`/`mulq` with fixed %rax/%rdx
constraints, `subq`+`setb` with a matching constraint and a `%b0` byte
modifier, `rm` for the memory-or-register operand.

That was measured, not assumed. All three helpers were compiled by tcc and
by gcc into one process and run against each other over 4,000,000
iterations of edge-biased random inputs (0, 1, ~0, ~0-1, 2^63, 2^63-1,
2^32, 2^32-1, and xorshift values; divisors normalized so h < d, since divq
faults rather than wrapping). Zero mismatches on all three.

There is corroborating evidence in tcc's own predefs: on FreeBSD, NetBSD,
OpenBSD and Darwin, tccdefs.h already defines __GNUC__, so tcc has been
taking this exact branch on four of its platforms all along. Linux is the
only target where it does not.

Widening the gate rather than leaving tcc on the portable path is what puts
tcc on the SAME configuration gcc builds and upstream tests. The
alternative -- asm enabled for the s2n routines but portable C for these
three helpers -- is a mix that no upstream build produces and nobody tests.
Fewer distinct configurations is the correctness argument here; the speed
of hardware divq is a secondary benefit.

## What this does not change

gcc and clang define __GNUC__, take the branch exactly as before, and see
an identical set of macros and inlines. The gcc-built libcrypto.so is
byte-identical across this patch.

--- a/dep/libressl/crypto/bn/arch/amd64/bn_arch.h
+++ b/dep/libressl/crypto/bn/arch/amd64/bn_arch.h
@@ -28,14 +28,12 @@
 
 #ifndef OPENSSL_NO_ASM
 
 #define HAVE_BN_ADD
 #define HAVE_BN_ADD_WORDS
 
-#define HAVE_BN_DIV_WORDS
-
 #define HAVE_BN_MOD_ADD_WORDS
 #define HAVE_BN_MOD_SUB_WORDS
 
 #define HAVE_BN_MUL_COMBA4
 #define HAVE_BN_MUL_COMBA6
 #define HAVE_BN_MUL_COMBA8
@@ -50,13 +48,25 @@
 
 #define HAVE_BN_SUB
 #define HAVE_BN_SUB_WORDS
 
 #define HAVE_BN_WORD_CLZ
 
-#if defined(__GNUC__)
+#if defined(__GNUC__) || defined(__TINYC__)
+
+/*
+ * Nothing on amd64 defines bn_div_words -- not the s2n-bignum assembly, not
+ * bn_arch.c. The only definition in the tree is the portable C one in
+ * bn_div.c, compiled under #ifndef HAVE_BN_DIV_WORDS. So HAVE_BN_DIV_WORDS is
+ * not a claim that some faster bn_div_words exists here; it is a claim that
+ * nothing will *call* one, which is true exactly when the inline below
+ * replaces bn_div.c's own bn_div_rem_words_inline -- that fallback is the sole
+ * caller. Defining it outside this conditional left a compiler that does not
+ * take this branch with the caller compiled in and the callee compiled out.
+ */
+#define HAVE_BN_DIV_WORDS
 
 #define HAVE_BN_DIV_REM_WORDS_INLINE
 
 static inline void
 bn_div_rem_words_inline(BN_ULONG h, BN_ULONG l, BN_ULONG d, BN_ULONG *out_q,
     BN_ULONG *out_r)
@@ -112,11 +122,11 @@ bn_subw(BN_ULONG a, BN_ULONG b, BN_ULONG *out_borrow, BN_ULONG *out_r0)
 	    : "cc");
 
 	*out_borrow = borrow;
 	*out_r0 = r0;
 }
 
-#endif /* __GNUC__ */
+#endif /* __GNUC__ || __TINYC__ */
 #endif /* _WIN32 */
 
 #endif
 #endif
